  "explanation": "Computer systems employ a memory hierarchy to balance cost and performance.  This hierarchy consists of different levels of memory with varying speeds and capacities.  Faster, smaller memories (caches) are closer to the processor, while slower, larger memories (main memory and disk) are further away.  Caching strategies aim to place frequently accessed data in faster levels of the hierarchy to reduce access time and improve overall system performance.  Effective cache management is crucial for optimizing program execution speed.",
